%description:

TcpCubic.cntClamp:invalid -- a negative clamp stops the run with an error.

The claim: the code accepts 0 and more.

The classes: linux (20, the default, the value of Linux) and tight (a smaller value), invalid
(below 0). The code: while no W_max is known, the ACK count of one increment is at most the
value (TcpCubic::update()).

Host under test: host1, with TcpCubic. Observation: the error, and the exit code of the run.

%# parameter: TcpCubic.cntClamp
%# class: invalid
%# value: -1
%# key: *.host1.tcp.conn-*.algorithm.cntClamp
%# expected-result: PASS

%activity:
EV << "TcpCubicCntClampInvalid compiled\n";

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
*.host1.tcp.conn-*.algorithm.cntClamp = -1    # the parameter under test
*.host1.tcp.tcpAlgorithmClass = "TcpCubic"
include ../../parameter/ini/_bulk.ini
include ../../parameter/ini/_base.ini

%exitcode: 1

%contains-regex: stderr
cntClamp must be 0 or more, but it is -1
